<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<!--
  The iOS half of the Tauri Info.plist merge (Epic 62, Story 62.6, FR-408).

  On every `tauri ios build` / `tauri ios dev` the CLI rewrites
  gen/apple/keeper_iOS/Info.plist as the merge of, in this order, later
  winning: the XcodeGen-generated file, the CLI's own keys, `Info.plist`
  beside this file (the macOS bundle's, written for a Mac), then THIS file
  (tauri-cli `mobile/ios/build.rs`, `merge_plist`). Without this file the
  iPhone's microphone dialog said "Everything is saved locally on this Mac",
  because the macOS string overwrote the one `project.yml` declares.

  Only keys the macOS file also carries belong here — every other iOS key
  lives in `gen/apple/project.yml` (AD-32), which is what `xcodegen
  generate` and a plain Xcode run see — with one exception, stated below.
  The sentences MUST equal the ones in `project.yml`: that copy is the
  project's truth, this copy is the override that reaches the bundle.
-->
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
	<key>NSMicrophoneUsageDescription</key>
	<string>keeper listens through the microphone only while you talk to a model you configured — after you tap Talk or say your wake phrase — and turns your words into text on this phone. Nothing is recorded or uploaded.</string>
	<!--
	  Story 63.4 gave the macOS bundle its own speech sentence, and the merge
	  carried it onto the phone: the installed IPA's prompt read "into text on
	  this Mac itself" on an iPhone, caught by install-ios.sh's own proof step
	  (2026-09-04). The same trap as the microphone key above, one epic later.
	  This string MUST equal the one in `gen/apple/project.yml`.
	-->
	<key>NSSpeechRecognitionUsageDescription</key>
	<string>keeper turns what you say into text on this phone, using on-device recognition only, so it can send your words to the model you chose. Your voice never leaves the device; keeper refuses server recognition.</string>
	<!--
	  The island (Epic 65, Story 65.5, AD-194). The macOS file does not carry
	  this key, so the merge alone would keep project.yml's — but this is the
	  one key without which `Activity.request` throws `unsupported` and the
	  whole story renders nothing, and install-ios.sh reads it off the
	  installed bundle as a proof. Stated in the last file the merge reads so
	  that no earlier step can drop it. Both copies say `true`.
	-->
	<key>NSSupportsLiveActivities</key>
	<true/>
</dict>
</plist>
